														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2119481">
									

<p>As someone who in a hipsterish manner can claim he did stuff like this before it was popular (though not in a nice manner like this - I just have references to numerous numbered documents and guidelines of the likes of <em>As material produced during the experiment appears to lack anomalous properties, its use is approved for non-critical applications as defined in document CSG-0371-N.</em>, I disagree.</p>
<p>Sure, it can be used as you say, which is usually stupid (as you say) but really, what referring to nonexistent documents does and should do is allow you not to write about things that would derail the article you're trying to write, but still retain realism by the act of implying that the information is readily available to whoever is reading the document in-universe (as in the above case would be usage guidelines for non-anomalous materials originating from unrelated anomalous processes).</p>
<p>Really, unlike expungements which can be a fairly powerful tool in terms of, well, eliciting response from a reader, this is just a bit of window dressing that doesn't need much attention. In Kate's articles, I outright liked this method as it was fresh and new, never mind the article names were fairly amusing (which is always a plus), but really we're giving the feature more attention than it deserves.</p>

